\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sa160\sl252\slmult1\i\f0\fs22 Library Manager Josh Murray announced that April is Arts Month and shared that the library is giving away Civic Arts Passports while supplies last. He invited children and families in the community to a special event, where members of the Teen Advisory Board will read their original story about an otter\rquote s quest to save the reef. The event will also include games and activities, and is open to families, teens, and kids on April 17, 2025, from 11:00 to 12:00 PM.\line\line Planning Manager Adam Finestone informed the community to a forum to learn about the Residential Overlay District (ROD) at the Joslyn Community Center on April 9, 2025, from 6:00 - 8:00 PM.\cf1\b\i0\line\line\cf0\b0\i Environmental Programs Administrator Britny Coker-Moen shared information about the City\rquote s Earth Month campaign, taking place throughout the month of April. Stamp cards featuring a variety of activities are available, with additional activities listed on the City\rquote s website at www.manhattanbeach.gov/earthmonth. All activities are free and open to the public. Participants can redeem their prizes at the Earth Day Celebration event, held at the Farmers Market behind the library on April 29, 2025. Additionally, there will be a morning Ocean Protection Tour on April 29, 2025, and RSVPs can be made through the City website.\cf1\b\i0\line\line\cf0\b0\i Planning Manager Finestone provided an update regarding Item No. 6 on the agenda, noting that the City has received several comments related to the Residential Overlay District (ROD). He clarified that the ROD is not on this evening\rquote s agenda and reminded the public that the ROD community forum will be held at the Joslyn Community Center on April 9, 2025, from 6:00 to 8:00 PM.\line\line Waste Management (WM) Recycling Education Representative Bobby Bell announced that the first paper shredding event of the year will take place at Mira Costa High School on April 26, 2025. Each resident or business owner may bring up to three banker boxes of paper for shredding.\line\line\pard Councilmember Franklin announced that last Tuesday, he represented the South Bay Cities Council of Governments by providing public comments to the Los Angeles County Board of Supervisors, as part of the next steps toward funding solutions for the unhoused. He shared that the Board has established a Local Solutions Fund, through which a portion of Measure A funds will be allocated to the South Bay Cities Council of Governments to help connect individuals with the services they need.\cf2\i0\f1\par
\par
